config KSU_KERNEL_UMOUNT
	bool "KernelSU kernel umount (nomount) support"
	depends on KSU
	default y
	help
	  Unmount mounts that KernelSU registered (for example module overlays)
	  when a process transitions to an untrusted app UID.  This is the
	  KernelSU Next "nomount" behaviour and keeps root-hiding tools such
	  as SUSFS from seeing stale mount points.

	  Note: because the nomount call sites live inside the KernelSU-Next
	  tree (drivers/kernelsu/hook/setuid_hook.c), this Kconfig knob is
	  declarative here - the init call is not gated without modifying
	  KernelSU-Next.  The runtime flag ksu_kernel_umount_enabled still
	  controls behaviour (defaults to on).

	  Say Y unless you manage mounts yourself.

config SUSFS
	bool "SUSFS (SU Hide File System) support"
	depends on KSU=y
	depends on BOOT_CONFIG
	default y
	help
	  SUSFS is a KernelSU Next extension that hides root access from apps
	  by virtualising paths, mounts, uname, kstat and other kernel
	  interfaces.

	  This links SUSFS into the kernel image; there is no
	  susfs_guard_lkm.ko and no runtime module load.  The standard
	  userspace SUSFS module drives it through the reboot(2) supercall.
	  It queries KernelSU's ksu_uid_should_umount through kallsyms, so
	  CONFIG_KALLSYMS_ALL should be enabled for the umount gate to take
	  effect.

	  Say N if you do not need root hiding.
